{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecificLimits.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecificLimits.Normed","lean:module:Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.InfiniteSum.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.InfiniteSum.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.AbsoluteValue.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Normed.Group.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Summable","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.quantumDilog_functional_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.quantumDilog_functional_eq","kind":"theorem","line":301,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/PhiQConvergence.lean#L301-L340","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.quantumDilog_multipliable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.quantumDilog_multipliable","kind":"theorem","line":200,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/PhiQConvergence.lean#L200-L265","doc":"**Brane-tower convergence half of Gap 4.**\nFor `1 < q` and `|z| < q`, the q-dilogarithm / q-Pochhammer product\n\n  Φ_q(z) = ∏_{k≥1} (1 - q^{-k} z)^{-1}\n\nis `Multipliable` — i.e. it converges to a well-defined non-zero real.\nEach factor is non-zero by `quantumDilog_factors_nonzero`, and the\ninverse-factor terms are summable by comparison with the geometric\nseries, so `Real.multipliable_one_add_of_summable` applies. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:quantum-dilog-convergence","label":"prop:quantum-dilog-convergence","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/quantum-dilog-convergence.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.quantumDilog_denom_multipliable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.quantumDilog_denom_multipliable","kind":"theorem","line":190,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/PhiQConvergence.lean#L190-L198","doc":"The denominator product `∏ (1 - q^{-(k+1)} z)` is `Multipliable`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.summable_quantumDilog_terms","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.summable_quantumDilog_terms","kind":"theorem","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/PhiQConvergence.lean#L183-L188","doc":"The terms `a k = q^{-(k+1)} z` are summable for `1 < q` (any `z`).\nThis is the geometric summability repackaged in the `* z` form used\nby the multipliability proofs below. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.quantumDilog_factors_nonzero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.quantumDilog_factors_nonzero","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/PhiQConvergence.lean#L111-L159","doc":"The product `∏ (1 - q^{-k-1} z)` is non-zero whenever\n`1 < q` and `|z| < q`. Together with summability, this proves\n`Multipliable (fun k => (1 - q^{-k-1} z)^{-1})` —\ni.e. `Φ_q(z)` is a well-defined positive real.\n\nThis is the analytic content underpinning\n`mass-endomorphism-tower.lean §QOU.MassEndomorphism.Arch.quantumDilog`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.summable_q_inv_geometric","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.summable_q_inv_geometric","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/PhiQConvergence.lean#L59-L89","doc":"For `1 < q : ℝ`, the geometric series `Σ_{k≥0} q^(-(k+1))`\nconverges absolutely. This is the foundation for Φ_q\nconvergence — every term in the q-Pochhammer / quantum\ndilogarithm is bounded by a multiple of `q^{-k}`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}